# 5. Very important: Ingress does NOT call Pods directly

The architecture is:

```text
Ingress
   │
   ▼
Service
   │
   ▼
Pod
```

Not:

```text
Ingress
   │
   ▼
Pod IP
```

For example:

```text
34.100.200.50
       │
       ▼
Ingress
       │
       │ /orders
       ▼
order-service
       │
       ▼
10.x.x.x Pod
```

The `order-service` Kubernetes Service takes care of finding the appropriate Pod.

---

# 6. How order-service talks internally

Your Kubernetes environment variables are:

```yaml
env:
  - name: PORT
    value: "3000"

  - name: USER_SERVICE_URL
    value: "http://user-service:3000"

  - name: PRODUCT_SERVICE_URL
    value: "http://product-service:3000"
```

So when the external request reaches:

```text
http://34.100.200.50/orders
```

the following happens:

```text
Client
  │
  │ /orders
  ▼
Ingress
  │
  ▼
order-service
  │
  │ http://user-service:3000/users/1
  ▼
user-service
  │
  ▼
User Pod


order-service
  │
  │ http://product-service:3000/products/101
  ▼
product-service
  │
  ▼
Product Pod
```

---

# 7. How do you know Ingress is working?

Run:

```bash
kubectl get ingress
```

Wait until:

```text
ADDRESS
34.xxx.xxx.xxx
```

Then:

```bash
curl http://34.xxx.xxx.xxx/users/1
```

If you get the user JSON, you've successfully achieved:

**Internet → GCP Load Balancer → GKE Ingress → Kubernetes Service → Node.js Pod**

Then test:

```bash
curl http://34.xxx.xxx.xxx/products/101
```

and finally:

```bash
curl -X POST http://34.xxx.xxx.xxx/orders \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d "{\"userId\":\"1\",\"productId\":\"101\",\"quantity\":2}"
```

